"""Grievance handling, conflict and stakeholder engagement."""


def build(course, module):
    return course(102, 'Grievance Conflict and Stakeholder Engagement',
        'Grievance, Conflict and\nStakeholder Engagement',
        'Design a grievance pathway that residents can use safely, with clear decisions, appropriate escalation and remedy follow-up.', [
        module('Design a usable grievance entry point',
            'Describe accessible reporting routes and safeguards for different kinds of concern.',
            """A grievance is an expression of concern that calls for attention or remedy. A mechanism is usable when affected people know it exists, can reach it, understand its process and can access advice or support where needed. The UN Guiding Principles describe effectiveness criteria that can be used to review a non-judicial mechanism. In practice, ask who is likely to be unable to use the first channel and what an accessible alternative would require. [S86]

Use several routes that match access and safety conditions, for example a staffed call line, an accessible meeting appointment or a trusted independent contact. Explain who will see the information and when a response can be expected. A person who raises a concern against a supervisor may need a channel outside their workplace. Avoid requiring the same data for process questions, individual harm concerns and requests for urgent support. Share confidentiality limits before collecting sensitive details. [S89]

Set an acknowledgement method and a realistic timeframe. Give each report a reference that can be used without repeating a sensitive narrative. Classify what the requester wants, such as a service referral, investigation, correction of a record or access to an appeal. Keep a way to route matters that belong with qualified services or public authorities. Maintain external options, and tell people about them when appropriate. Review patterns in access rather than publishing details that could identify individuals. [S86]

Test the mechanism through contrasting fictional users. Can a temporary worker on an evening shift enter a concern privately and receive a response while away from work? Can a person without reliable internet learn the next step? Record the failed steps with a named person to address them.""",
            'The complaint desk opens during a single work shift and passes every report straight to a supervisor who may be named in it.',
            'Design two entry routes and a minimal intake record. Add an acknowledgement check and an alternative route for conflicted cases.',
            'Give practical access at different times and an independent route. Ask only for necessary information and protect follow-up details.',
            """
Design for access. | A complaint channel requires internet and some workers lack access. Which change addresses the barrier? | Offer a private offline route with an equivalent response process. | Increase the website font only. | Require everyone to obtain a personal device. | Count offline workers as uninterested. | Equivalent routes improve practical accessibility.
Minimise intake. | A resident needs to correct an appointment record. What information is appropriate at entry? | Information needed to locate and correct the relevant record through the agreed route. | A full personal history for every concern. | Contact details of all household members. | The person’s unrelated medical information. | Collect information in proportion to the issue.
Explain sharing. | A person raises a concern about their supervisor and fears repercussions. What should they learn before submitting details? | Who sees the report, confidentiality limits and protected alternatives. | Only the number of complaints received last year. | The supervisor’s usual shift pattern. | A guarantee that no information could ever be disclosed. | People need practical, accurate information to choose a safe route.
Confirm entry. | What is the best evidence that a complainant can track the next step? | Receipt with a usable reference, safe contact and a response timeframe. | A case folder created with no identifier given. | A notice saying complaints are welcome. | An internal chart that the person cannot see. | Acknowledgement should make the process navigable.
Preserve alternatives. | A local mechanism is unavailable for a complaint requiring external authority. What should the practitioner do? | Explain appropriate external routes and support permitted referral. | Require completion of every internal step before offering any information. | Treat the matter as outside all possible remedy. | Forward it publicly to invite a response. | Appropriate external routes remain available.
"""),
        module('Use voluntary dialogue with safeguards',
            'Decide whether dialogue is appropriate and prepare a fair process with separate escalation criteria.',
            """Dialogue can help parties understand a dispute about access, service schedules or commitments. Begin by asking what each party needs and what decision is genuinely open. Identify any power imbalance and whether participants can decline safely. If the matter includes intimidation, serious harm, coercion or a need for formal investigation, consult the applicable safeguarding and referral process. Do not bring people into a joint session when it would increase risk or compromise their rights. [S86]

For a suitable low-risk issue, prepare each participant separately. Clarify purpose, scope, confidentiality and the role of the facilitator. Arrange language support and schedule enough time for participants to obtain advice. Give a participant a chance to pause or return to the process later. Make clear who can agree to an operational change. Do not treat willingness to attend as agreement with the final proposal. [S89]

In the session, distinguish interests from requested outcomes. A trader who asks for a gate to reopen may need access during early trading hours. Options could include a time-limited alternative or a review of the route. Compare each against the interests and evidence without disclosing sensitive details unnecessarily. Arhin’s two-community research provides a reason to ask how resources and power affect reported consent. Use local evidence about who can reasonably exercise choice. [R95]

Write outcomes in plain language and verify each participant's understanding. Preserve disagreement and independent review where warranted. Record what the responsible body will do and when it will be checked. If a voluntary route no longer remains safe or credible, stop it and use the relevant referral or escalation pathway.""",
            'Two market groups disagree about delivery hours. Separately, a worker reports that a supervisor threatened shifts for people who complain.',
            'Decide which matter may suit voluntary dialogue, which needs protected escalation and what must be in the facilitator’s mandate.',
            'Compare schedules through a voluntary process if safe. Handle the retaliation report separately through an appropriate protective route.',
            """
Screen suitability. | A worker fears reprisals for privately raising a concern. How should the report be handled first? | Use a protected route and assess safety before considering dialogue. | Require a joint session immediately. | Publish the allegation in meeting minutes. | Treat the concern as a routine scheduling dispute. | A process can intensify risk when power and safety are unresolved.
Check voluntariness. | What best shows a participant can freely use a dialogue process? | They can decline or pause it without a penalty and know other available routes. | They are scheduled by a supervisor. | Their attendance was listed in a public notice. | The facilitator asked them to attend once. | A real choice requires practical ability to refuse.
Separate interest and position. | A vendor asks for the gate to reopen, explaining that early deliveries enable trade. What is the underlying interest? | Reliable delivery access during viable trading hours. | A particular gate opening as the sole interpretation. | The title of the gate supervisor. | A public vote on all schedules. | Examining interests can make more options visible.
Check mandates. | The facilitator can record proposals but cannot change opening hours. What should be clear? | Who can approve changes and when referral or review will happen. | All suggestions took legal effect as recorded. | The facilitator will approve the change privately. | The parties need no information about the decision-maker. | A process is credible when authority and timing are known.
Record non-agreement. | Two groups try dialogue and retain different views. What should the record show? | Disagreement, open options and relevant next review routes. | A unanimous agreement because both groups attended. | Only the organiser’s preferred view. | The withdrawal of every prior concern. | Attendance and discussion do not establish agreement.
"""),
        module('Verify remedy and learn from recurring failures',
            'Write decision, appeal and remedy checks that distinguish closure from whether harm was addressed.',
            """A grievance response needs a reason linked to the concern and the available evidence. Explain what was decided, what will be done, the expected timing and who can review the response. Provide an accessible review route for procedural or substantive concerns. A sign-off from the original handler may show that a step was taken. An independent check matters when a conflict or disputed outcome could affect trust. Keep a dated record of significant corrections. [S86]

Define the requested and agreed remedies. A rerouting complaint could lead to a service redesign, while some households may also need assistance during a gap. Track milestones such as approved, implemented and verified working. Ask the affected person or group whether they could use the remedy at the relevant time. Check independent records when appropriate. A closure count can include withdrawn, redirected or unresolved concerns. Use distinct categories and preserve their meaning. [S89]

Protect privacy when analysing patterns. Give internal reviewers only the data they need. Public summaries can report safe aggregate information about issues handled, delays, repeated barriers and action dates. Small counts or descriptions can disclose identity even without names. Decide how to record and review sensitive trends without publishing case details. Provide affected people with updates through agreed channels. [S86]

Use recurrence to guide improvement. If different groups report the same hard-to-use route, assess what the process design requires and record an accountable change. Name the schedule and measure for retesting it. Check whether an apparent increase in complaints reflects deteriorating conditions, improved access to the mechanism or both.""",
            'A dashboard counts 20 cases as closed. Eight were withdrawn, and residents say the replacement access route never opened.',
            'Rewrite the dashboard categories. Define a remedy evidence check, a safe update and a recurrence signal for corrective review.',
            'Separate process dispositions from outcomes. Verify route operation with authorised records and affected users, then keep unresolved cases visible.',
            """
Explain the decision. | What makes a grievance decision useful to the person who raised it? | Reasons, agreed next action, timing and a review route. | A status code with no explanation. | The number of unrelated closed cases. | A reference to all staff email messages. | The explanation allows the person to understand and challenge the outcome.
Distinguish withdrawal. | Eight reports were withdrawn and twelve had remedies implemented. How should these appear? | Report the dispositions separately and verify remedy outcomes. | Report 20 verified remedies. | Report only referrals and discard both counts. | Combine all cases under evidence of restored access. | Case dispositions do not establish equivalent results.
Verify use. | A manager signs off a replacement path. What further evidence shows it addresses the complaint? | Affected people can use it under the agreed conditions and timing. | The work order status turned green. | The path appears in a proposal. | More complaints were assigned after it opened. | Remedy effectiveness concerns whether the change addressed the grievance.
Protect small counts. | A public report’s category could reveal one person’s identity. What is appropriate? | Keep a protected review record and use a safe aggregate in public. | Publish it alongside the person’s work shift. | Remove the case completely from internal tracking. | Ask all residents to confirm who reported it. | Separate accountability from avoidable disclosure.
Interpret recurrence. | Complaint volume rises after a new accessible entry route opens. What should be checked? | Changes in access, issue frequency and who can report. | Conclude harm necessarily doubled. | Close the new route automatically. | Compare totals without the change date. | A volume change can reflect access, underlying problems or both.
"""),
    ])
